    "q-339551": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339551",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi )=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's try direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not defined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258704/assets/ec58b5df3cbc58a83e356399c0a772ee905507e090f14c3350988c727c13136f.png)\n\nWe see that as $x$ approaches $\\frac{1}{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi )$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi )=-\\infty .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
